00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:19.440
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.320
Why don't you give us sort
of a high-level overview

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.440
of like, what's the problem
that Knative is trying to solve?

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:26.190
VILLE AIKAS: Sure.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.356
So one of the things that
we did with the Kubernetes

00:05:28.356 --> 00:05:29.430
was we really--

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:30.660
this is my words.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:35.340
I think we gave the cloud
and the developers assembly

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.074
language for the cloud.

00:05:37.074 --> 00:05:38.490
So you want to go
and get stuff up

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:40.660
and running in the cloud,
the instruction set,

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.260
if you will, and
provided Kubernetes.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.090
And Kubernetes is great.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:46.920
Problem is that
there's a lot of pieces

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.320
that we had to go ahead
and understand in order

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.030
to effectively use it.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.880
And for the most part, the
developers don't really

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.550
need to know all
those things, ideally,

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.800
but they are exposed
to it in order for them

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.060
to actually get
things up and running.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.220
So as we looked
around for a while,

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.270
we realized that there is
a need for a higher level

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.670
abstraction at
Kubernetes level that

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:11.460
would allow us to go ahead and
meet the developers' need much

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:12.570
more closely.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:15.060
And that's sort of
where the Knative comes.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.630
So if you think about the
Kubernetes and even Istio

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:20.475
as well as kind of
the assembly language,

00:06:20.475 --> 00:06:22.950
Knative is bringing you
something like the [? Lipsy ?]

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.159
or standard [? lip. ?]
That's how I think about it.

00:06:25.159 --> 00:06:26.700
So there's a lot of
the problems that

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.950
are being solved all over
the place, especially

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:30.907
in the serverless
space, and we kind of

00:06:30.907 --> 00:06:33.240
wanted to bring all of our
friends and all the knowledge

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.280
and solve those
problems in one place,

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.050
and then go ahead
and focus on building

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.480
super awesome developer
experiences on top of that.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:40.950
MARK CHMARNY: If I could just
maybe add something to this,

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.408
I think there should
be no surprise

00:06:42.408 --> 00:06:45.510
to the regular audience
of this podcast

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:48.130
that Google, they're big
on Kubernetes and Istio.

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:49.130
MARK MANDEL: Little bit.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:51.130
MARK CHMARNY: If we kind
of look a little higher

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:53.820
up the stack where the
developers of applications

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.422
on Kubernetes actually are,
I think, to some degree,

00:06:56.422 --> 00:06:57.630
they just want to write code.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.796
They don't want to deal
with building Docker images,

00:06:59.796 --> 00:07:02.257
uploading them to registry,
deploying different services,

00:07:02.257 --> 00:07:03.840
exposing them to the
internet, and all

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.050
the other mechanical
things that are related

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:07.460
to deploying an application.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.470
So at the end of the
day, really Knative

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:12.459
solves for the experience of
the developer on Kubernetes

00:07:12.459 --> 00:07:14.750
that would not require them
to know every single aspect

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:15.860
of what Kubernetes is.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:20.300
And the analogy that Ville uses
around the low-level kernel,

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:21.012
it's spot on.

00:07:21.012 --> 00:07:22.970
It's kind of something
that we're hoping nobody

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:24.345
ever has to think
about when they

00:07:24.345 --> 00:07:27.157
are developing applications
on Knative, what's underneath.

00:07:27.157 --> 00:07:28.490
GABI FERRARA: I have a question.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:31.070
So you say that you
don't want developers

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:34.856
to be worrying about every
aspect of Kubernetes, of how

00:07:34.856 --> 00:07:36.740
to create a load
balancer, for instance.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:38.912
Knative would solve
that problem for you?

00:07:38.912 --> 00:07:41.370
Like, they are load balancer
[INAUDIBLE] to expose the load

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:41.550
balancing--

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:42.190
MARK CHMARNY: Yeah.

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:43.460
GABI FERRARA: --on Kubernetes.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.946
And my question I ask,
because a lot of the problems

00:07:46.946 --> 00:07:48.320
that people have,
it's because we

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:52.500
need to create a Docker image,
so you have a YAML file there,

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.650
then you have YAML
file for Kubernetes.

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:58.000
And does Knative
solve that problem,

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.860
to have to deal with
different configuration files

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:02.189
to do just one deployment?

00:08:02.189 --> 00:08:03.980
MARK CHMARNY: That's
an excellent question.

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.580
I'm sure Ville can
speak to that too,

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.038
being closer to
the technical side.

00:08:07.038 --> 00:08:09.560
But on the developer
side, I think the things

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:12.890
that you will see that directly
speak to that kind of friction

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:17.960
that you just described is being
able to skip the entire Docker

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.150
image as a requirement
for somebody who

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:21.410
wants to use Knative.

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:25.070
So we have use cases
[? where ?] address directly

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.680
deploying from a source
code, and Knative still

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:30.169
builds the Docker image
for you behind the scene,

00:08:30.169 --> 00:08:32.419
but you, as a developer,
don't have to worry about it.

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:34.580
Furthermore, just like
you have pointed out,

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.866
once you deploy that
thing, usually people

00:08:36.866 --> 00:08:39.240
are excited because the Hello
World application comes up.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.650
But if you're looking at kind
of day two or day five problems

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:44.749
where the application
is now successful,

00:08:44.749 --> 00:08:46.790
and dealing with some of
the autoscaling problems

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:48.581
that developers normally
have to deal with,

00:08:48.581 --> 00:08:50.748
this is another area where
Knative really helps you,

00:08:50.748 --> 00:08:52.455
because you don't have
to think about it.

00:08:52.455 --> 00:08:53.750
It's based on autoscaling.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:56.610
And when you're
audience goes away,

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:58.850
so does the resources
that are required to run

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:01.330
your application in Knative.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:02.330
GABI FERRARA: Thank you.

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:03.390
MARK MANDEL: Now this
sounds really cool.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.390
So just to take a step back
a little bit in some ways.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.515
Like, we said sort of like,
we're deploying things.

00:09:07.515 --> 00:09:08.790
We're deploying applications.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:10.730
Like, what does
that mean to you?

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:13.100
Am I like, deploying
Redis with this?

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:15.399
Or what kind of
things am I deploying?

00:09:15.399 --> 00:09:16.940
VILLE AIKAS: That's
a great question.

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:17.440
Yeah.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.440
So typically, the first target
market really for Knative

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:22.940
is what is typically
the serverless, a.k.a.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:25.310
Stateless services.

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:27.140
It is things like functions.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.930
You want to make sure that
you have a very similar user

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.870
experience for both of
the developer experience,

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.810
for both of these, whether it's
an application, or a function,

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:38.420
so you don't have to go and
have that mental shift, which

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:40.370
is, oh, I'm doing
function-y stuff,

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:42.380
or I'm doing
application-y stuff,

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:45.260
when typically the lifecycle
and the development processes

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:47.499
and practices are
very, very similar.

00:09:47.499 --> 00:09:49.790
You have some code that I
would like you to run for me,

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.085
and don't screw it up, please.

00:09:51.085 --> 00:09:51.960
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.660
Are there particular
languages that

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:55.340
are supported in
Knative now, or can I

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:56.730
bring my own stack if I want?

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:57.640
VILLE AIKAS: Bring
your own stack.

00:09:57.640 --> 00:09:57.890
MARK CHMARNY: Yeah.

00:09:57.890 --> 00:09:59.570
That's the beauty of Knative.

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:01.930
The word that we keep
using to describe that

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:03.350
is this idiomatic experience.

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:06.160
If you were developing
applications in Node, Java,

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.270
Python, C#, you bring that
same exact experience,

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:14.760
and you don't change your day to
day operations to kind of adapt

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:15.650
to Knative.

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:17.510
You follow the
same procedures you

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:19.640
would develop your
application somewhere else.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.659
There is no extra
attributes you have to put

00:10:21.659 --> 00:10:22.700
on top of your functions.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:24.825
There is no extra configuration
inside of your code

00:10:24.825 --> 00:10:27.230
that you have to do in order
to make it work for Knative.

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:29.600
Knative is kind of
built on the assumption

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.560
that there is a serverless
container that we will run,

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.990
and we also hope you would
build in that container.

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.750
To a large degree, Knative is
polyglot from that standpoint.

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:40.550
Anything that can
be containerized

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.310
can be run on Knative,
as long as it's,

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:44.920
right now, HTTP application.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:45.920
VILLE AIKAS: Absolutely.

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:46.190
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:48.140
So I can write functions in,
like, Haskell or something.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:48.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:49.780
VILLE AIKAS: If that's
what you like, yes.

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:50.890
GABI FERRARA: Even PHP?

00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:52.291
VILLE AIKAS: Even PHP.

00:10:52.291 --> 00:10:54.332
GABI FERRARA: I know not
a lot of people like it,

00:10:54.332 --> 00:10:55.130
but like, even PHP?

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:55.760
MARK CHMARNY: Yes.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:56.300
Yes.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:56.630
GABI FERRARA: I like it.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:57.590
MARK CHMARNY: Even PHP.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:58.464
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:10:58.464 --> 00:11:02.061
So if I want to build like,
a little Knative application,

00:11:02.061 --> 00:11:03.560
what does that
experience look like?

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:04.370
How does the tooling work?

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:05.660
What does that flow look like?

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:08.030
MARK CHMARNY: Knative kind of
has two different entry points,

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:08.580
so to speak.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.240
For people kind of familiar
already with Kubernetes itself,

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.600
and they've been kind of used
to interacting with kubectl,

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:17.750
the command line for,
the CLI, for Kubernetes,

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:19.832
you can just follow the
exact same methodology.

00:11:19.832 --> 00:11:22.040
You build your application,
you build your container,

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.440
and you deploy it, except
that when it lands,

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:26.750
it kind of lands as
a Knative service

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:29.635
that inherits a lot of the
properties of the platform I

00:11:29.635 --> 00:11:31.760
just explained, which is
like autoscaling and being

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:33.880
able to be configured
dynamically, and so forth.

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:35.480
Your routes management.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:38.660
If, however, you're coming
to Knative as a developer who

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:41.297
has not worked on top
of Kubernetes before,

00:11:41.297 --> 00:11:43.380
there's a growing number
of tools in the community

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:46.460
to kind of enable you to
deal directly with the code,

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:49.770
and not have to build
images yourself.

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:52.120
So the use case of
a source to a URL

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:54.350
is one that we
often point to where

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:56.660
you develop the application,
for example, in Go.

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:59.960
And using the build pipelines
that are developed inside

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.780
of Knative, you can
actually execute a pipeline

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:04.970
which will build that
code from your repository,

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:09.440
private or public, on Git
or GitHub, inside that code

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:11.250
and build that image
for you and deploy it.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.708
So all of that is happening on
the cluster, which gives you

00:12:13.708 --> 00:12:16.370
a lot of benefits around
kind of obfuscating data,

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:19.040
or using the resources only
during the build phase,

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:21.590
and then being able to use
them to host your application

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:22.670
after the fact.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:23.570
MARK MANDEL: Is
there tooling as well

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:25.111
for, like, local
development, or do I

00:12:25.111 --> 00:12:26.870
have to push everything
up to the cloud

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:28.214
every time I'm working?

00:12:28.214 --> 00:12:30.380
MARK CHMARNY: We do have
some initial implementation

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:33.200
of Knative on top of Minikube.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:35.540
Obviously, some of
the requirements

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:38.240
that come with
Istio and Kubernetes

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.020
are a little more
resource hungry.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:42.860
But we have people who have
successfully done that,

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:45.140
so you could actually imagine
running the entire stack

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:46.014
on your local laptop.

00:12:46.014 --> 00:12:46.570
Yes.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:47.900
VILLE AIKAS: But in most
cases, you don't even

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.270
need the entire stack,
because you can [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:52.520
or just stay in
local web server,

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:54.320
or run only unit
tests and integration

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:56.690
tests and everything else,
and then the kinds of things

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:58.490
that you might not
care about when

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:00.530
you do a local development,
like, well, does

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:02.510
its scale to zero, does
it go away, it's like,

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:03.345
no, no, no, it does.

00:13:03.345 --> 00:13:04.220
Don't worry about it.

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.595
Just push it in the cloud,
and we'll handle that for you.

00:13:06.595 --> 00:13:08.480
So that's kind of the
operational aspect

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:11.577
that we hide for you that you
typically don't necessarily

00:13:11.577 --> 00:13:13.910
have to worry about when
you're doing local development.

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:15.326
But as Mark pointed
out, yeah, you

00:13:15.326 --> 00:13:18.590
can do that with the
Minikube or anything else.

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:21.800
MARK CHMARNY: And GCP provides
developers with a free tier,

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.640
and I think with
our latest release,

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.550
we actually have a
model in which you could

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.840
use the free tier to run small
instance, single node instance,

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:31.960
of Knative on GKE.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:35.350
GABI FERRARA: We list
on our Knative website

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:38.090
four features in
the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:42.890
mainly serving build events
and serverless [INAUDIBLE] GKE.

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:45.260
I have a question
regarding about events.

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:48.470
We say here that it would
be a universal subscription,

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:50.540
delivery and
management of events,

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:54.230
would be that similar
to a Pub/Sub service,

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:55.970
or it's a different
kind of event?

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:58.279
What do you mean
by events in here?

00:13:58.279 --> 00:13:59.820
VILLE AIKAS: That's
a great question.

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:03.560
So eventing typically lends
itself very well to Knative

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:05.810
execution environment, because
it's serverless, right?

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:07.760
So if no events are
happening, aka, a tree

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.560
falls in the forest,
nobody hears about it.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:10.310
Right?

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:13.604
But the eventing is a
piece that says, look,

00:14:13.604 --> 00:14:15.270
we want to have
[? the very ?] principle

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:16.830
to understand the events.

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:19.700
So in a Pub/Sub event,
for example, happens,

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:23.090
or somebody publishes
an event into a Pub/Sub,

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:25.430
you want to say, please
execute this function for me.

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:26.600
And yes, we support that.

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.280
That was the direct
answer to your question.

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:30.113
But in addition to it,
it might be something

00:14:30.113 --> 00:14:32.210
that somebody pushed
some code to GitHub,

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:36.680
or somebody uploaded an image
to Google Cloud Storage, or S3,

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:39.710
or whatever else, where
there's an action,

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:41.610
some other system
happened, and you

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.520
want to decouple
those in such a way

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.734
that you don't have to
understand, how did it happen?

00:14:46.734 --> 00:14:47.900
You just say, you know what?

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:50.570
When something happens over
there, let me know about it.

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:53.240
So the eventing, you can
go ahead and describe

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:54.720
what these events look like.

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:57.170
And then you can go and
describe strategies, a.k.a.,

00:14:57.170 --> 00:14:59.310
what should happen
when that event fires.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:00.950
So if you want to
do something simple,

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:04.530
like thumbnailing, which is
a pretty classic example,

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:07.760
you would say, OK, when somebody
uploads an image into a Cloud

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.070
Storage bucket, please
go ahead and run

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:11.429
this little piece of code.

00:15:11.429 --> 00:15:13.470
So that's what the eventing
concerns itself with.

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:14.190
GABI FERRARA: Thank you.

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:14.550
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:17.049
And does that mean as well that
you can extend that eventing

00:15:17.049 --> 00:15:17.580
mechanism?

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:18.770
So if you want to
have your own custom

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:20.780
events for, I don't
know, random things that

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:22.880
happen inside maybe your
application or something,

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:24.110
could you respond to those?

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:24.890
VILLE AIKAS: Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:26.840
That's one of the things
that makes me super excited,

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:29.360
is the fact that I do think
that this model of computing

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:31.820
is going to be made so
much more approachable

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:34.657
to different people, if you have
a very rich eventing ecosystem.

00:15:34.657 --> 00:15:36.740
So the more events you
have, the more desire there

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:38.850
is to go and process
those, and so forth.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:41.577
So being able to go ahead
and create new event sources

00:15:41.577 --> 00:15:43.910
is something that we have
been thinking about very hard,

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:46.250
and making sure that
that is very, very easy,

00:15:46.250 --> 00:15:49.040
and it behaves and looks
the same everywhere.

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:51.710
So I personally would like
to go ahead and make sure

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:53.540
that you, as a
developer, again, don't

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:56.420
have to worry about reading
a whole bunch of instructions

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:59.349
and clicking and dragging and
copying and pasting for to say,

00:15:59.349 --> 00:16:00.640
hey, just give me those events.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:02.780
'Cause that's what I care
about as a developer,

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:04.080
not about how does it happen.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:05.900
I say, give me some events.

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:07.525
GABI FERRARA: So you
don't need to have

00:16:07.525 --> 00:16:09.740
a [INAUDIBLE] running
inside Knative

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:11.360
to be able to get my events.

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:15.080
I can use Knative itself to
manage those events for me?

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:15.830
VILLE AIKAS: Yes.

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:17.330
MARK CHMARNY: One
of the nice things

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:19.760
that I like to talk about
eventing that often registers

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.940
with the community, or the
developers outside, who often

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:24.980
don't deal with Knative
on a daily basis,

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:27.830
is the decoupling
of the event source

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:29.460
from the actual
processing of events.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:31.640
So when you are
writing something

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:34.970
that's supposed to act when
it's triggered by event,

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:37.520
you really don't
have any awareness

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:40.610
or any code in your
little processor

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:42.980
that is aware of that, which
means that you can bind

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:46.340
that processor and processing
function to a number

00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:50.030
of different events, as long as
that message type is defined.

00:16:50.030 --> 00:16:51.860
And this is where
Knative also is

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:55.820
pretty innovative with regards
to adopting CloudEvents, which

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:58.220
is kind of a common
way of defining events

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:01.530
across the internet,
which is strongly typing

00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:04.490
the different properties
that each event will have,

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:07.069
while allowing for extensions
of the different data

00:17:07.069 --> 00:17:08.819
components that come with that.

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:11.349
So Knative supports
CloudEvents 0.2

00:17:11.349 --> 00:17:13.640
right now, which is the
current version of CloudEvents.

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:14.089
VILLE AIKAS: Yep.

00:17:14.089 --> 00:17:15.950
MARK CHMARNY: And this is where
the industry is collaborating

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:17.616
and making sure that
we are able to talk

00:17:17.616 --> 00:17:20.060
to each other as the
number of different sources

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:21.210
increases over time.

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:23.300
MARK MANDEL: Is that, like,
an open standard or something?

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:24.050
MARK CHMARNY: Yes.

00:17:24.050 --> 00:17:26.140
It is actually a CNCF,
I believe, project.

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:27.890
MARK MANDEL: Oh, I
didn't know about that.

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:28.160
All right.

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:30.230
So from the other side,
and you touched on it

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:31.669
a little bit earlier--

00:17:31.669 --> 00:17:33.710
so as a developer, I'm
like, this sounds awesome.

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:34.280
I love it.

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:36.080
But how do I install this?

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:36.830
How do I run this?

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:38.996
Like, what does the operator
experience kind of look

00:17:38.996 --> 00:17:39.740
like here?

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:41.960
MARK CHMARNY: Because this
is an open-source project,

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:46.310
you actually can take the
installation guides that

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:49.820
are already published in a
repository and deploy Knative

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:53.390
to any Kubernetes-certified
instance of a service.

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:57.439
So, obviously, GCP has a great
Kubernetes service called GKE.

00:17:57.439 --> 00:17:59.480
But other cloud service
providers also have that.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:02.880
You can also install to your
own Kubernetes on-premise.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:05.060
So the decision would
be basically based

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:07.200
on the fact of two factors.

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:12.470
One is the locality, as in, do
you need to have applications

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:15.140
somewhere close, located
to your data source that's

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.720
not available somewhere
else, or something like that,

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:19.670
or to the operational model?

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:23.450
If you are already managing
your own Kubernetes cluster,

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:25.070
installing Knative
on top of that

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:28.580
would be potentially a nominal
set of scripts that you

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:30.140
install, and be done with that.

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:32.460
However a growing
number of companies

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:36.060
are actually not interested in
operating their own Kubernetes

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:36.580
cluster.

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:38.760
They like the
common API, and they

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:41.370
like that kind of certification
across different cloud service

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:45.180
providers, but being able
to deploy those applications

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:48.240
on top of that service,
and not be locked

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:49.500
into a particular provider.

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:53.760
So Knative in that case
works very well with GKE.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:57.490
At Next last year-- so it's
been probably seven months--

00:18:57.490 --> 00:19:01.020
we've launched GKE
add-on that, by including

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:04.350
one additional flag or
one checkbox during the UI

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:07.620
provisioning, you can bring
the entire Knative stack that's

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:10.920
been validated to work for
that particular version of GKE.

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:13.890
So out of the box, you're
getting the full, Knative stack

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:15.630
that's already
configured for you,

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:18.240
and you're ready to develop your
first application as soon as

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:19.630
that stack is available.

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:22.034
GKE currently takes
about, what, three minutes

00:19:22.034 --> 00:19:23.200
to provide the entire stack.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:26.460
So you have a pretty quick
provisioning capability

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:27.020
for yourself.

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:29.020
GABI FERRARA: Back tracking
a bit on the example

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:33.600
that Mark gave, oh, that means
I can run Redis on Knative,

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:39.060
and Ville said, no, it's more
stateless applications, right?

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:42.660
So what kind of
application or code

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:48.400
should I not be running
inside of Knative?

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:51.930
I'm a big advocate for
not running databases

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:55.040
inside Kubernetes,
because working

00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:57.580
in stateful applications
is complicated.

00:19:57.580 --> 00:19:59.594
So is that true
also for Knative?

00:19:59.594 --> 00:20:01.510
VILLE AIKAS: So let me
make sure I understand.

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:03.930
So you're saying that stateful
applications shouldn't

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:05.060
be running on Knative?

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:06.310
GABI FERRARA: I'm asking that.

00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:07.440
VILLE AIKAS: Yeah,
so typically not,

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.570
because one of the
things that we want to do

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:12.790
is provide the pay
as you go model,

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:14.760
so that resources
are not being used.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:16.560
Typically, most
stateful applications

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:20.230
require you to have something
up and running all the time.

00:20:20.230 --> 00:20:25.320
However, because Knative is
built on top of Kubernetes,

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:26.820
one of the things
that you can do

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:31.740
is that you can run all these
things alongside your Knative,

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:34.330
and you just reach out to
the Kubernetes directly.

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:36.660
So I know while you might
not be a fan of running

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:39.960
stateful applications
even on Kubernetes,

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.870
you do have that
option, because there

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:46.040
is a lot of great abstractions
in the Kubernetes [INAUDIBLE],,

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:48.280
you know, like
standardizing disks

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:49.530
and everything else like that.

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:50.080
GABI FERRARA: Yes.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:51.663
VILLE AIKAS: So with
things like that,

00:20:51.663 --> 00:20:54.060
you can go ahead and get
the best of both worlds,

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:54.690
if you will.

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:56.190
It's the developers
that really want

00:20:56.190 --> 00:21:00.690
to focus on the stateless
applications and functions.

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.240
They can go ahead and
run in the same cluster.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:05.100
They just have a
different API that

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:08.604
is a higher level that targets
only the things that they

00:21:08.604 --> 00:21:10.020
should really have
to worry about.

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:10.610
GABI FERRARA: Thank you.

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:11.850
MARK MANDEL: And that
actually probably

00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:14.260
segues nicely into what I'm
thinking about, which is,

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:16.800
so if I'm running the
managed version of Knative,

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:19.620
do I still have access
to a Kubernetes API?

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:21.780
Like, the Kubernetes cluster
that it's working on?

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.279
Or is that completely hidden
from me at that point in time?

00:21:24.279 --> 00:21:26.070
MARK CHMARNY: It is
still available to you.

00:21:26.070 --> 00:21:28.740
When you check that extra
box or include, then,

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:31.380
one extra flag during
the gcloud command

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:33.570
to provision the cluster,
you're really not

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:35.550
losing any of that
Kubernetes capability.

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:38.200
You're adding one extra
item on top of that.

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:40.110
So you still have
that Kubernetes API

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:41.490
if you wanted to.

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:43.530
You can interact
directly with Knative

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:46.510
for the applications that
don't require the entire stack.

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:50.490
But we're trying to not
hide any of the capabilities

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:52.980
that Kubernetes has because,
obviously, it's popular,

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:55.930
and it's really being adapted
by a lot of different companies.

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:57.630
So there's no point
of doing this.

00:21:57.630 --> 00:21:59.820
But in the same time,
for the developers who

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:03.690
don't need to know the
entire scope of the schema

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:07.800
for a particular deployment
or service, kind of creating

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:10.620
a very well-defined
principle objects

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:14.335
that are easy to understand for
a developer, service, route,

00:22:14.335 --> 00:22:15.440
a configuration.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:17.220
It's something that,
just by understanding

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:18.720
the meaning of the
word, you kind of

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:20.190
know what it's supposed to do.

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.650
Great credit goes to the
initial thought leaders on this,

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:24.066
like Ville and
others, who kind of

00:22:24.066 --> 00:22:25.990
thought about, what
would I call that?

00:22:25.990 --> 00:22:27.990
What would I need to do
if I was a web developer

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:29.950
and wanted to create
something there?

00:22:29.950 --> 00:22:33.150
So I think that has proven
to be a very elegant way

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:35.880
of addressing those common
problems that developers have.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:38.730
But if you are not able
to accomplish something

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:41.730
that you need, you're
not falling very far.

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:44.420
The Kubernetes itself is
still there underneath.

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:47.450
It gives you a comfort to be
able to adapt to the use cases

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:49.242
that Knative is, perhaps,
not designed for.

00:22:49.242 --> 00:22:50.200
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:51.780
If either of you
had to choose one

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:54.930
of your favorite features out of
Knative, what would you choose?

00:22:54.930 --> 00:22:56.430
MARK CHMARNY: Oh,
that's a good one.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:57.060
Ville?

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:58.920
VILLE AIKAS: I
like scale to zero.

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:02.280
I'm always coming up with rando
things, and I spin things up,

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:05.217
and then I forget about
them, and then I come in,

00:23:05.217 --> 00:23:07.050
and I don't have to
remember to go clean up.

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:12.190
So if I start some jobby
job somewhere, and go home,

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:14.280
I don't want to halfway
through home remember

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.200
like, oh, no, I
left the stove on.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:17.620
My house is going to burn down.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:19.660
It's like, Knative's
going to just clean it up.

00:23:19.660 --> 00:23:21.560
GABI FERRARA: Or
a surprise bill.

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:21.780
VILLE AIKAS: Yeah.

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:22.571
Or a surprise bill.

00:23:22.571 --> 00:23:23.869
Yes, exactly.

00:23:23.869 --> 00:23:24.660
MARK CHMARNY: Yeah.

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:27.300
You know, it's hard
to beat autoscaling.

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:30.850
We often list this on a same
level like many other features.

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:34.860
But I think it often goes
under-appreciated by even

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:37.650
the users of Knative,
because it's just there.

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:40.335
And when you don't look
at it, it's not there.

00:23:40.335 --> 00:23:42.710
But when you look at it or
somebody else is looking at it

00:23:42.710 --> 00:23:43.850
and using it, it's there.

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:45.070
It's pretty powerful.

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:46.778
I think the other
thing I would highlight

00:23:46.778 --> 00:23:50.820
there is that when we give
people the power of Knative,

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:54.570
as in the easy deploying
applications, and people coming

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:56.700
from microservices
world were starting

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:59.460
to deploy 20 to 30 different
applications on top of that,

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:01.464
quickly realizing that
maybe not all of them

00:24:01.464 --> 00:24:03.130
need to be exposed
to the outside world.

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:05.820
Maybe we should just kind of
expose one UI application,

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:09.330
or a service endpoint for API,
and then hide everything else.

00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:12.360
And I think with the recent
release of 0.3 a week

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:16.950
or two weeks ago of Knative,
we've added to this cluster

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:20.220
local provisioning for services,
which, with a single label,

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.590
single attribute in a
configuration, you're saying,

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:25.620
this is only to be used by
other services in this cluster,

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:27.480
not by anybody outside.

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:29.610
People already are
talking about appreciating

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.710
this very much, because
it allows them a lot more

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:33.420
flexibility with the
type of applications

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:34.710
they're deploying on Knative.

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:36.250
MARK MANDEL: I want to dig
a little deeper on the scale

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:36.840
to zero part.

00:24:36.840 --> 00:24:38.490
This is something I've
actually just remembered

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:40.364
I've always been thinking
about with Knative.

00:24:40.364 --> 00:24:42.930
So in a traditional Kubernetes
world, you have pods,

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:44.470
and you have nodes
in your cluster.

00:24:44.470 --> 00:24:47.460
But if you have scale
to zero on the functions

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:50.100
within Knative, what happens
to the nodes in that cluster?

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:52.020
Do you scale the nodes to zero?

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:52.985
How does that work?

00:24:52.985 --> 00:24:53.735
VILLE AIKAS: Yeah.

00:24:53.735 --> 00:24:56.830
So in that case, we do not
spin the nodes down to zero.

00:24:56.830 --> 00:24:58.700
So the Knative doesn't
do that for you.

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:00.800
So we just take care
of the pods for you,

00:25:00.800 --> 00:25:03.240
so you would have to do
something in addition

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:05.760
to go and actually scale
the cluster itself properly,

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:07.520
to go and bring the
nodes in and out.

00:25:07.520 --> 00:25:08.311
MARK CHMARNY: Yeah.

00:25:08.311 --> 00:25:11.940
Knative will deal with
optimizing the efficiency

00:25:11.940 --> 00:25:13.037
of using your cluster.

00:25:13.037 --> 00:25:13.870
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:25:13.870 --> 00:25:15.990
MARK CHMARNY: And by
creating more demand

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:19.080
on the physical resources, it
gives the signal to Kubernetes

00:25:19.080 --> 00:25:20.750
to scale horizontally.

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:22.610
So Kubernetes will
see the pressure,

00:25:22.610 --> 00:25:25.350
will provision a new
node, and it's probably

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:28.372
not something that dynamically
will happen transparently

00:25:28.372 --> 00:25:30.330
to the users, who suddenly
need to be offloaded

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:31.560
to another node.

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:33.480
But even then, it's
getting better and better

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:35.610
with how fast that can be
done behind the scenes.

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:36.960
MARK MANDEL: So you can
use the Kubernetes node

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:38.160
autoscaler in conjunction?

00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:38.730
That works?

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:39.270
MARK CHMARNY: Yes.

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:41.280
If your cluster is
configured for autoscaling,

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:43.975
Knative will benefit from that,
just by running on top of that.

00:25:43.975 --> 00:25:44.850
VILLE AIKAS: Exactly.

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:45.450
MARK CHMARNY: So the
only other thing,

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:48.170
maybe perhaps adding here, in
addition to those features,

00:25:48.170 --> 00:25:50.932
I kind of feel bad for
identifying the cluster

00:25:50.932 --> 00:25:52.140
[INAUDIBLE] and nothing else.

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:53.556
Some of the things
that we've done

00:25:53.556 --> 00:25:58.650
in the GKE version of Knative--
so if you provision Knative

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:01.362
using the GKE add-on, you're
obviously getting the Knative

00:26:01.362 --> 00:26:02.820
components, but
you're also getting

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:08.400
a lot of additional service
integration from GCP itself.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:10.050
You're getting
Stackdriver integration

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:13.190
for logging, some tracing
information, you get there.

00:26:13.190 --> 00:26:14.550
You get some monitoring.

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:17.820
So increasingly, this feels
like a fully managed service,

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:19.920
even though the
open-source components,

00:26:19.920 --> 00:26:21.900
you have to do a lot
of this yourself.

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:25.290
In case of GKE add-on,
it feels like something

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:27.720
that you can kind of say,
Google, this is your problem.

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:28.569
Solve that for me.

00:26:28.569 --> 00:26:31.110
Don't page me in the middle of
the night when one of the pods

00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:32.160
doesn't start.

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:33.654
I will just write
my applications.

00:26:33.654 --> 00:26:35.070
GABI FERRARA: Ville
and Mark, what

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:38.700
do you expect in the future
of Knative, either open source

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:40.020
or on GCP?

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:41.187
What we should expect?

00:26:41.187 --> 00:26:42.770
VILLE AIKAS: One of
the things that we

00:26:42.770 --> 00:26:46.530
are working very, very hard is
making the developer experience

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:47.210
easy.

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:49.110
That part, I hope we
have convinced you of.

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:51.359
The other thing that we are
putting a lot of work into

00:26:51.359 --> 00:26:54.750
is making sure that the Knative
stack itself is manageable.

00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:56.790
So we want to make
sure that when

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:59.370
our operator friends,
either whether it's

00:26:59.370 --> 00:27:02.370
at Google or some other
companies, that are running

00:27:02.370 --> 00:27:05.550
and providing the Knative stack,
that they have the confidence

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:07.380
that the stack can
be managed, which

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:10.170
means you can go ahead and
have seamless upgrades.

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:13.087
When new versions roll out,
you a high degree of confidence

00:27:13.087 --> 00:27:15.420
that everything is going to
go and just keep on running,

00:27:15.420 --> 00:27:16.660
and everything else.

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:19.680
So that's where we are spending
a lot of effort right now.

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:21.930
That is not really
developer-facing.

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:23.640
But it's very, very
important to me

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:26.190
personally, is to ensure
that the stack itself

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:27.420
can be managed.

00:27:27.420 --> 00:27:30.060
GABI FERRARA: Just a question
that pops up now in my head

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:31.570
when you were explaining that.

00:27:31.570 --> 00:27:31.880
MARK CHMARNY: Uh-huh?

00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:34.255
GABI FERRARA: I said that's
going to manage new versions,

00:27:34.255 --> 00:27:35.460
upgrades.

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:38.940
Let's say, one thing like
Spectre happens again.

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:42.600
With Knative, would I have
to go manually patching

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:45.840
all my servers, or it just is
going to do everything for me,

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:48.600
and I don't have to
worry about that?

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:50.320
I just thought about
that, because we

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:51.840
did have that problem recently.

00:27:51.840 --> 00:27:54.690
Not we, as we as
an IT community.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:56.490
And that was a big problem.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:57.890
Would Knative help in that?

00:27:57.890 --> 00:28:00.210
VILLE AIKAS: Knative itself
wouldn't really help.

00:28:00.210 --> 00:28:02.550
But it would be taken
advantage, basically,

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:04.650
of the cluster operators
that are providing

00:28:04.650 --> 00:28:05.740
the underlying cluster.

00:28:05.740 --> 00:28:09.240
So for example, our
tireless Google SRE folks

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:12.130
would just make sure
that it just goes away.

00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:13.680
So Knative, no.

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:16.830
But because we are building
on top of Kubernetes,

00:28:16.830 --> 00:28:19.390
then everything that we get
from Kubernetes and managed

00:28:19.390 --> 00:28:22.095
Kubernetes properly is
free out of the box.

00:28:22.095 --> 00:28:23.970
MARK MANDEL: Really
standing on the shoulders

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:25.254
of giants, as we say.

00:28:25.254 --> 00:28:26.670
VILLE AIKAS: That's
exactly right.

00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:28.440
MARK CHMARNY: And
in GKE, now we have

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:32.030
those flags for auto
repair and auto upgrade.

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:33.620
So if those two
options are checked

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:35.630
when you provision
to Knative, you

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:37.790
are benefiting from
that very thing.

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:40.010
You will get control,
[? plane ?] upgrade,

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:41.992
and you will get auto
repair of the nodes.

00:28:41.992 --> 00:28:42.950
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:45.660
VILLE AIKAS: I think coming
back to the future of Knative,

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:48.860
just doubling down on little
more on what Ville was saying.

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:52.040
With 0.3 release, I
think we really have not

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:53.510
talked about it
this way publicly,

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:55.220
but maybe we should
a little more.

00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:56.720
It's probably the
very first release

00:28:56.720 --> 00:28:58.752
that kind of solves for
a lot of the friction

00:28:58.752 --> 00:29:00.960
points that we've gotten
feedback from the community.

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:04.790
So people, when we first
announced 0.1 release

00:29:04.790 --> 00:29:07.220
during Next in San
Francisco, there

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:09.012
was a few customers
that actually

00:29:09.012 --> 00:29:10.970
were using that at that
time, but the community

00:29:10.970 --> 00:29:12.678
was still not providing
a lot of feedback

00:29:12.678 --> 00:29:15.800
of actual real-world use cases.

00:29:15.800 --> 00:29:17.450
Over the last seven
months, we actually

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:19.850
started getting
meaningful feedback

00:29:19.850 --> 00:29:21.770
from actual users,
people who run

00:29:21.770 --> 00:29:23.899
that for workloads
that, you know,

00:29:23.899 --> 00:29:25.940
I'm not sure if they're
production, but something

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:29.190
that is ready to provide some
kind of meaningful feedback.

00:29:29.190 --> 00:29:32.240
With 0.3 release, we've
actually implemented

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:34.911
many of the solutions for
many of the friction points

00:29:34.911 --> 00:29:36.660
that we've heard about
from the community.

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:40.340
So you will see kind of
that emphasis going forward,

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:43.700
going all the way to the
proverbial 1.0 release, which

00:29:43.700 --> 00:29:45.500
we will hopefully have soon.

00:29:45.500 --> 00:29:47.960
No timelines to speak of yet.

00:29:47.960 --> 00:29:49.970
I think that emphasis
on making sure

00:29:49.970 --> 00:29:51.830
that people are able
to leverage that

00:29:51.830 --> 00:29:54.800
to build real-world applications
that are not just Hello

00:29:54.800 --> 00:29:58.100
Worlds, but something that
helps them solve real problems,

00:29:58.100 --> 00:30:01.322
it's something that's very
close to the entire team, both

00:30:01.322 --> 00:30:03.280
on engineering, as well
as on the product side.

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:06.830
We make sure that those things
are addressed or, first of all,

00:30:06.830 --> 00:30:09.710
