00:31:58.940 --> 00:31:59.850
Question of the week.

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:03.080
How long does it take for
Cloud SQL to detect an outage

00:32:03.080 --> 00:32:05.300
and trigger high
availability failover?

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:06.230
How good is our stuff?

00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:08.435
GABI FERRARA: So short
answer, 60 seconds.

00:32:08.435 --> 00:32:09.430
[DING]

00:32:09.430 --> 00:32:12.812
But a lot happened to
that to be detected.

00:32:12.812 --> 00:32:13.520
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:32:13.520 --> 00:32:15.890
GABI FERRARA: So every
second, the primary instance,

00:32:15.890 --> 00:32:18.830
or your master, depending
on the terminology you use,

00:32:18.830 --> 00:32:22.670
sends a write to our system
database, which does heartbeat.

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:25.340
And if it doesn't attack
a heartbeat signal

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:29.990
for 60 seconds, it will
trigger the failover,

00:32:29.990 --> 00:32:33.779
and the standby instance will
be the new primary instance

00:32:33.779 --> 00:32:34.820
that you're hiding there.

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:38.280
So you always have a hot
standby replica there.

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:41.600
And that's how long it takes
to activate the failover.

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:44.360
Now, the failover may take a
bit more, because it depends

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:46.430
on the replication, and
that's a lot of stuff

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:48.135
that we can cover
in another podcast.

00:32:48.135 --> 00:32:49.010
